-  sept
'  shrine_types: the shared vocabulary between the versioned
'  namespace (shrine) and its clients (lain) — the kernel record
'  (myth), the committed version with its versioning information
'  (saga), and the diff unit (note).
+  twig
  '  One slot-value pair: the unit a myth is built from.
  :  slot=path =pail
+  myth
  '  The record at a node: slots (paths, used as opaque keys) to
  '  pails.  The unit of state the namespace versions and a behavior
  '  manipulates.  A myth holds a handful of slots, so it is an
  '  ordered map (/mop), not a tree — and a type alias, so the whole
  '  mop method set (get/put/size/uni) applies directly.
  mop[path pail lt_pith]
  +  new
    '  The empty myth.
    ^  myth
    (mop/empty)
  +  sing
    '  The one-slot myth.
    \  slot=path v=pail
    ^  myth
    |  mop/sing[path pail lt_pith] slot v
  +  meld
    '  Union; the add side wins collisions (the default talk law).
    \  add=myth cur=myth
    ^  myth
    cur.uni(add)
  +  add
    '  Chain one twig onto a myth: mit.add(twig).
    \  =twig mit=myth
    ^  myth
    mit.put(twig.slot twig.pail)
+  lock
  '  One care's two independent local counters.  Data advances for
  '  every changed version of the care; shape advances only when the
  '  care's live domain changes.  Shape implies data, but is not a
  '  position in the data timeline.
  :  data=nat shape=nat
+  track
  '  One care's local clock and its dense data-case history.  Epochs
  '  maps local data case -> sovereign commit epoch; shape is a count
  '  only and has no historical query axis.
  :  =lock epochs=uon[nat]
+  ever
  '  The three path-local care histories for one namespace path.
  '  Only explicitly asserted paths (live records or tombstones) own
  '  one.  Transparent structural spines never acquire a ledger.
  :  x=track y=track z=track
+  yuga
  '  A node's versioning information: one lock per care, plus the
  '  apollo time and the PKI blockheight at mint.
  :  x=lock
     y=lock
     z=lock
     now=nat
     block=nat
+  oath
  '  The security envelope: signature and hash chain.  Zeroed in
  '  this port — validity lives a layer out.
  :  sig=nat prev_hash=nat
+  aeon
  '  Versioning plus security: everything about a committed version
  '  except the record itself.
  :  =yuga =oath
+  saga
  '  The committed version of a node: the aeon and the record.
  '  /sov/step adjusts the yuga as it applies diffs, so historical
  '  case queries are floor lookups, not subtree scans.
  '  The record rides behind a pin: each committed myth is one
  '  content-addressed unit, so snapshotting the sovereign
  '  re-serializes spines, never stored records (global.plan's
  '  small-pins discipline).  Version-only re-mints (ass/next over
  '  the held record) share the old pin verbatim.
  :  =aeon myth=pin[myth]
+  note
  '  One diff: replace the record, merge into it, or retract the
  '  node.  Versioning is the namespace's job — a diff carries none.
  +  make
    :  =myth
  +  poke
    :  =myth
  +  cull
    :
  &  note/make
     note/poke
     note/cull
+  care
  '  Read restriction: the node only (.x), the node plus its frontier
  '  — the nearest record-bearing descendants, transparent paths passing
  '  through (.y) — or the whole subtree (.z).
  &  care/x
     care/y
     care/z

  +  x
    :

  +  y
    :

  +  z
    :

  +  seg
    '  The care's /h path segment.
    '  ?=  ((care/seg .z) ($ts "z"))
    \  c=care
    ^  iota
    ?  c
     > .x  'x
     > .y  'y
     > .z  'z
+  hunt
  '  A cared path: the (care, path) pair that names both a place and
  '  a reach — .x the node alone, .y the node plus its direct kids,
  '  .z the whole subtree.  A face's rendering claim is one, riding
  '  as DATA at ['hunt] on the face record itself (a pail slot, so
  '  it is explorer-visible and pokeable live): p is spelled
  '  relative to the face's own mount root ([] = the root itself),
  '  so the claim survives mounting anywhere and needs no rebase.
  :  c=care p=path
+  safe
  '  A version-pinned cared read: the target's PER-CARE local DATA
  '  case (not a sovereign epoch), the care, and the path.  A safe
  '  names an immutable answer — its /h spelling (safe/spell) never
  '  changes meaning once minted — so a record slot holding one is
  '  a pinned reference: a citation when left alone, a LIVE LINK
  '  when the record carries the fresh kook, which keeps every
  '  top-level safe slot current as its target changes.
  :  ver=nat =care =path

  +  spell
    '  The /h path a safe dereferences through:
    '  /h/<care>/<ver>/<len>/<path>.
    '  ?=  ((safe/spell (safe 3 .x ['a 'b])) [($ts "h") ($ts "x") ($u 3) ($u 2) ($ts "a") ($ts "b")])
    \  s=safe
    ^  path
    =  head=path  ['h (care/seg s.care) ($u s.ver) ($u s.path.len)]
    head.weld(s.path)

  +  of_hunt
    '  A safe over a hunt's (care, path) at a known case.
    \  ver=nat h=hunt
    ^  safe
    |  safe ver h.c h.p
' Structural typing *of* the namespace.  Node-level enforcement is
' LIVE: a record carrying its lash at ['sys 'lash] is judged by the
' commit gate (lain_poke/guard_diffs via lain_poke/fit_check) — a write whose
' final record breaks its own contract rejects the whole event,
' except a write that CHANGES the lash (adoption over legacy data
' is allowed; the watch app's standing queries find the survivors).
' Soma/yoke subtree patterns and the dep side (fief) remain
' vocabulary only for now.
'
+  curb
  '  The acceptable types for one slot: the value must inhabit one
  '  of these types (a union, not a conjunction).  Types are named
  '  by their namespace paths.
  row[path]
  +  of_type
    '  A nominal row contract from compiler reflection. The constructor
    '  supplies its defining module path under either compiler.
    \ reflected=type
    ^ curb
    ?> (./rec brand items) reflected []
    ? (eq brand 0) []
    = target
        % offset=nat 0 start=nat 0 parts=path []
        ^ path
        ? (ge offset (wid brand))
          ? (eq start offset) parts
          parts.snoc(($ts (loadvar start (sub offset start) brand)))
        ? (eq (load8 offset brand) 47)
          = next
              ? (eq start offset) parts
              parts.snoc(($ts (loadvar start (sub offset start) brand)))
          | _ offset.inc offset.inc next
        | _ offset.inc start parts
    [target]
+  curbs
  '  Slot -> curb typing for one record.  Types only what is
  '  present; presence lives in lash/need.  Open: slots absent from
  '  the map are unconstrained (records stay row-polymorphic).
  mop[path curb lt_pith]
+  lash
  '  R/W typing for the record at one name.  need is CNF presence:
  '  each inner row is a disjunction of slots (at least one present);
  '  the outer row conjoins the groups.  [[status]] = status required;
  '  [[lede about]] = lede-or-about.  lede and help document the
  '  name this lash specs — a one-line description and longer prose,
  '  text cords, 0 = absent (not /lore: that lives downstream in
  '  pact).
  :  read=curbs
     write=curbs
     need=row[row[path]]
     lede=nat
     help=nat
  +  new
    '  The empty lash: no typing, no presence demands, no docs.
    ^  lash
    |  lash (mop/empty) (mop/empty) [] 0 0
+  zeta
  '  Pattern over one path segment: a literal iota, or a typed
  '  variable constrained to an aura.
  &  zeta/lit
     zeta/var

  +  lit
    :  i=iota

  +  var
    :  aura=nat
+  soma
  '  A pattern-trie of typing requirements over a subtree: an
  '  optional lash here plus zeta-edged arms.  Matching precedence
  '  is lit over var; at most one var arm per node.
  :  fil=maybe[lash] kids=row[soma/arm]
  +  arm
    :  pat=zeta sub=soma
  +  new
    '  The empty soma: no lash here, no arms.
    ^  soma
    |  soma .none []
  +  step
    '  The sub-soma one segment selects: a lit arm on iota equality
    '  wins outright; the var arm admits only a matching aura and
    '  yields when no later lit claims the segment.
    \  seg=iota s=soma
    ^  maybe[soma]
    %  j=nat 0
       var=maybe[soma] .none
    ^  maybe[soma]
    ?  (ge j s.kids.len)  var
    =  a  s.kids.at(j)
    ?  a.pat
     > (./lit i)
       ?  (eq_iota i seg)  (./some a.sub)
       |  _ j.inc var
     > (./var tag)
       ?  (eq tag (aura_tag seg))  (_ j.inc (./some a.sub))
       |  _ j.inc var
  +  at
    '  The lash this soma assigns to a path: one /step per segment,
    '  the landing node's fil.  .none off the trie or at a lash-less
    '  node.
    \  pax=path s=soma
    ^  maybe[lash]
    %  i=nat 0
       cur=soma s
    ^  maybe[lash]
    ?  (eq i pax.len)  cur.fil
    <  sub  (soma/step pax.at(i) cur)
    |  _ i.inc sub
+  op
  '  One declared operation on a spec'd record: poke-with-an-overlay.
  '  fn is the operation itself — (fn input cur) computes the WHOLE
  '  next record, the input riding the event and never the
  '  namespace (lain's writ/warp applies it and commits the result
  '  as a replace; the gate judges the final record as usual).  The
  '  receiver and output name roles; the inputs document the input
  '  record's slots, types as documentation spellings.
  :  name=iota
     receiver=iota
     inputs=row[[slot=path ty=nat]]
     output=iota
     lede=nat
     fn={\ myth myth}myth
+  deed
  '  Options for dependency injection.
  :  req=bool
+  fief
  '  A dependency: injection options, the read restriction it
  '  needs, and the shape it requires of the target.
  :  =deed
     =care
     =soma
+  yoke
  '  A whole namespace spec: the record at the root, pattern-typed
  '  kids, and concrete-path deps.
  :  self=lash
     kids=row[soma/arm]
     deps=axal[fief]
  +  at
    '  The lash a yoke assigns to a spec-relative path: the root's
    '  own lash at [], the kid trie below.
    \  pax=path y=yoke
    ^  maybe[lash]
    ?  (eq 0 pax.len)  (./some y.self)
    |  soma/at pax (soma .none y.kids)
  +  adopt
    '  Seed records with their spec'd contracts adopted: every seed
    '  whose path this yoke lashes gets the lash at ['sys 'lash].
    '  The mounting write is itself the adoption (contract-changing
    '  writes are gate-exempt), so every LATER write to the node is
    '  judged by the commit gate.  The empty lash is skipped —
    '  adopting no constraints would only add noise.
    \  y=yoke seeds=row[[p=path t=myth]]
    ^  row[[p=path t=myth]]
    =  one
         \  seed=[p=path t=myth]
         ^  [p=path t=myth]
         =  [p t]  seed
         ?  (yoke/at p y)
          > (./some l)
            ?  (equal l lash/new)  [p t]
            [p t.put(['sys 'lash] l)]
          > .none
            [p t]
    seeds.map(one)

' Overlays: persistent lenses over the namespace
' these ctors should on the /sys/weft slot with a descriptor at /sys/typp
' These cases are important to distinguish because cases need to be assigned
' to the result of overlays. Each of these ctors has a different "case algebra"
' i.e. the way that cases are assigned to the newly overlaid data
'
' The relative performance expense is roughly
' dyed < knit < felt < sewn
' '
+  knit
  ' build structure
  :  get={\ myth}axal[myth] patch=maybe[{\ myth}axal[myth]]
+  felt
  ' delete structure. inverse of +knit
  :  get={\ axal[myth]}myth patch=maybe[{\ axal[myth]}myth]
+  dyed
  ' transform content
  :  get={\ myth}myth patch=maybe[{\ myth}myth]
+  sewn
  ' transform structure
  :  get={\ axal[myth]}axal[myth] patch=maybe[{\ axal[myth]}axal[myth]]
+  spun
  ' transform structure with the view node's own record as the args
  ' channel: the /x lens ctor.  get receives the node's record (its
  ' slots ARE the view's constant args) and the tack target's tree
  ' pinned at y care, and returns the derived tree. /sys/weft holds
  ' this ctor inline or an x-care safe to a ctor record. /sys/tack
  ' holds a y-care safe. The defining datum owns the result case.
  :  get={\ myth axal[myth]}axal[myth]
     patch=maybe[{\ myth axal[myth]}axal[myth]]
+  typp
  ' registration for an overlay
  : name=nat
